<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsaparang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsaparang is the ruined capital of the kingdom of Guge, built on a ridge above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> valley in Zanda County, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. Its palaces, temples and cave dwellings rise up an eroded hillside, and its temples hold fine murals of the 15th and 16th centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-andrade">António de Andrade</a> founded a mission there in 1624, and the city declined after Ladakh conquered Guge around 1630.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsaparang.jpg" alt="Tsaparang" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: yuen yan · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ngari_Tsaparang_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
